From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: bind outbound SOE flows in the PPE

Every SOE SA is created with its PPE path disabled, so every IPsec
packet makes a round trip through the CPU: a QDMA TX request to the
SOE, a completion on the SOE RX ring, and the outer LOCAL_OUT or the
inbound XFRM path. This round trip limits the IPsec rate, sends most
outbound requests through the deferral worker and loses completions
under load.

Enable the PPE path of outbound SAs. The flowtable then binds the flows
of an outbound SA in the PPE with the SOE hop, and the PPE hands their
packets to the SOE and forwards the encrypted result itself. Packets of
a flow that is not bound yet and packets that the PPE sends to the CPU
still take the CPU path, and the bind barrier holds the binding of a
flow until the CPU requests in flight have completed.

Outbound SAs that use UDP encapsulation keep the CPU path.

Inbound SAs stay PPE-disabled. The PPE binds an inbound SA as a single
entry keyed on its outer ESP tuple that carries the egress of the first
inner flow, so the packets of the SA would no longer pass the inbound
policy check, the firewall or conntrack, the entry would outlive that
flow, and anti-replay would rest on the replay window of the engine,
which software never sees. Inbound ESP therefore stays with software
XFRM and the SOE CPU path.

Lower the number of requests an SA may have in flight from 7 to 4. The
limit of 7 followed the 3-bit completion sequence that is no longer
written to the hop descriptor. Fewer requests in flight lose fewer
completions without lowering the rate of the CPU path. The same limit
decides when inbound ESP is left to software XFRM.

Bound outbound entries make two teardown paths reachable that only
matter when an SA owns PPE flows:

- When the bound entries of an SA cannot be invalidated before the SA
  is freed, keep its slot reserved. A stale entry still carries the
  slot as its SA index and would otherwise feed the next SA programmed
  into that slot, possibly one toward another peer.
- airoha_ppe_soe_forget_sa() now unlinks every flow of the SA from the
  PPE hash lists, not only the inbound owner entries. A forgotten flow
  keeps the SOE fields in its entry data, and a later learn event would
  otherwise commit them as a plain entry, outside the bind barrier and
  without an SA.

The deferral worker, which now also carries the requests that the bind
barrier holds back, submits a deferred request after it has released
seq_lock and holds only a plain reference on the XFRM state.
xfrm_dev_state_flush() deletes and frees the offload state of an SA that
is still referenced, so a device going down could free the SA between
the dequeue and the submit. Take an offload reference on the state for
the submit, keep it until a request that has to wait again is back on
the queue, where the SA teardown finds it, and drop the request when the
state is no longer valid. xfrm_dev_state_free() waits for that
reference before the driver frees the SA.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -2545,12 +2545,10 @@ void airoha_ppe_soe_forget_sa(struct air
 		struct airoha_flow_table_entry *e;
 
 		e = container_of(flow, struct airoha_flow_table_entry, soe_flow);
-		if (e->type == FLOW_TYPE_SOE_INPUT_OWNER) {
-			spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
-			if (!hlist_unhashed(&e->list))
-				hlist_del_init(&e->list);
-			spin_unlock_bh(&ppe_lock);
-		}
+		spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
+		if (!hlist_unhashed(&e->list))
+			hlist_del_init(&e->list);
+		spin_unlock_bh(&ppe_lock);
 		airoha_ppe_soe_flow_release(flow);
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_soe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_soe.c
@@ -170,7 +170,7 @@
 #define AIROHA_SOE_HOP_FLAG_DECRYPTED 4
 #define AIROHA_SOE_HOP_INFO_ENCRYPT 2
 #define AIROHA_SOE_HOP_INFO_DECRYPT 3
-#define AIROHA_SOE_SA_INFLIGHT_MAX 7
+#define AIROHA_SOE_SA_INFLIGHT_MAX 4
 #define AIROHA_SOE_CTX_TIMEOUT HZ
 #define AIROHA_SOE_CTX_EXPIRE_INTERVAL (AIROHA_SOE_CTX_TIMEOUT / 2)
 #define AIROHA_SOE_SA_COOLDOWN (2 * AIROHA_SOE_CTX_TIMEOUT)
@@ -1889,7 +1889,7 @@ static int airoha_soe_alloc_sa(struct ai
 	new_sa->x = x;
 	new_sa->index = i;
 	new_sa->cmd = cfg.cmd;
-	new_sa->ppe_disabled = true;
+	new_sa->ppe_disabled = !(cfg.cmd & AIROHA_SOE_SA_CMD_ENC) || x->encap;
 	spin_lock_init(&new_sa->lock);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&new_sa->tx_queue);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&new_sa->rx_queue);
@@ -2511,6 +2511,7 @@ static void airoha_soe_bind_tx_work(stru
 		struct xfrm_state *x;
 		struct sk_buff *skb;
 		unsigned long flags;
+		bool held;
 		int err;
 
 		spin_lock_irqsave(&soe->seq_lock, flags);
@@ -2536,10 +2537,12 @@ static void airoha_soe_bind_tx_work(stru
 		x = ctx->tx.deferred_x;
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&soe->seq_lock, flags);
 
-		err = airoha_soe_submit_prepared_skb(sa, dev, skb, ctx, x,
-						     true);
-		if (!err)
-			continue;
+		held = xfrm_dev_offload_get(x);
+		if (held)
+			err = airoha_soe_submit_prepared_skb(sa, dev, skb, ctx,
+							     x, true);
+		else
+			err = -ENOENT;
 		if (err == -EAGAIN) {
 			spin_lock_irqsave(&soe->seq_lock, flags);
 			ctx->tx.deferred_skb = skb;
@@ -2549,8 +2552,13 @@ static void airoha_soe_bind_tx_work(stru
 			soe->bind_tx_queued++;
 			soe->bind_tx_draining = true;
 			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&soe->seq_lock, flags);
+			xfrm_dev_offload_put(x);
 			return;
 		}
+		if (held)
+			xfrm_dev_offload_put(x);
+		if (!err)
+			continue;
 
 		XFRM_INC_STATS(xs_net(x), LINUX_MIB_XFRMOUTERROR);
 		ctx->tx.deferred_skb = NULL;
@@ -3250,6 +3258,7 @@ static void airoha_soe_xfrm_state_free(s
 				"failed to invalidate PPE flows before freeing SA %u; keeping IPsec PPE path disabled: %d\n",
 				state->sa->index, err);
 			airoha_ppe_soe_forget_sa(state->dev->eth, state->sa);
+			state->sa->quarantined = true;
 			airoha_soe_ppe_adopt_quiesce(state->soe,
 						     state->ppe_quiesced);
 			airoha_soe_ppe_adopt_quiesce(state->soe,
